lume>357</volume>:<fpage>47</fpage>&#x02013;52.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1315422</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib9"><citation citation-type="journal">Hamman, B.D., J.C. Chen, E.E. Johnson, and A.E. Johnson. <year>1997</year>. The aqueous pore through the translocon has a diameter of 40-60&#x000c5; during cotranslational protein translocation at the ER membrane. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>89</volume>:<fpage>535</fpage>&#x02013;544.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9160745</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Hanein, D., K.E.S. Matlack, B. Jungnickel, K. Plath, K.-U. Kalies, K.R. Miller, T.A. Rapoport, and C.W. Akey. <year>1996</year>. Oligomeric rings of the Sec61p complex induced by ligands required for protein translocation. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>87</volume>:<fpage>721</fpage>&#x02013;732.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8929540</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Harris, C.R., and T.J. Silhavy. <year>1999</year>. Mapping an interface of SecY (PrlA) and SecE (PrlG) by using synthetic phenotypes and in vivo cross-linking. <source>J. Bacteriol.</source> <volume>181</volume>:<fpage>3438</fpage>&#x02013;3444.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10348856</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">High, S., S.S.L. Andersen, D. G&#x000f6;rlich, E. Hartmann, S. Prehn, T.A. Rapoport, and B. Dobberstein. <year>1993</year>. Sec61p is adjacent to nascent type-I and type-II signal-anchor proteins during their membrane insertion. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>121</volume>:<fpage>743</fpage>&#x02013;750.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8491769</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Johnson, A.E., and M.A. van Waes. <year>1999</year>. The translocon: a dynamic gateway at the ER membrane. <source>Annu. Rev. Cell Dev. Biol.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>799</fpage>&#x02013;842.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10611978</pub-id></citation></re